window.docContents[187].resolve({"/Hyperbolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Hyperbolic-Equations--Nonlinear-First-Order-Equations--Characteristics-and-Local-Existence--Characteristic-Equations-and-Compatible-Data":{"contents":"The characteristic system evolves the spatial point, the candidate gradient,\nand the value of the solution in one phase space. A classical solution induces\nthis system, while the converse construction needs compatible boundary data\nand a noncharacteristic initial surface.\n\nFor a phase-space state , the characteristic vector field isThus an integral curve  solves Evans's three equations (11) as one\nautonomous ODE. The field is defined on all of phase space; the PDE\nconstraint and compatibility of the initial data are imposed separately.\n\nThe field is chosen so that the graph of a classical solution is invariant\nunder its flow: along a curve in the base moving with velocity , the\nvalues and gradients of a\n solution obey exactly these equations.\n\nFor a  phase-space Hamiltonian, if a  solution satisfies the\nequation on an open set, then at every parameter value where a characteristic\nlies in that set its position,\ngradient, and value satisfy Evans's characteristic ODE identities.\n\nDifferentiate , , and\n along a curve with velocity . The chain rule gives\nthe equations for  and ; differentiating\n in the spatial directions and substituting the Hessian term\ngives the equation for .\n\nConversely, a characteristic triple need not come from a solution. What\nsurvives is the constraint:  is a first integral of the characteristic\nfield, so the hypersurface  is invariant under its flow.\n\nAt a point where the phase-space Hamiltonian is differentiable, every\ndifferentiable characteristic triple  satisfiesThe theorem is pointwise. If these hypotheses hold on an open interval containing zero, then\n there by the mean value theorem, so compatible initial data with\n stay on the constraint hypersurface. For a continuously differentiable\nHamiltonian, the interval form holds for every characteristic curve; see the automatic flow below.\n\nApply the chain rule to  and substitute the three\ncharacteristic equations. The , , and  contributions\ncancel pairwise, so the derivative is zero and the residual is constant.\n\nStarting characteristics from boundary data requires an initial gradient,\nwhich the data alone do not determine: the trace prescribes only the\ntangential components of , and the equation itself supplies one more\nscalar relation. Compatibility records both requirements.\n\nFor a parametrized initial surface  and prescribed trace , a\ncandidate initial gradient  is compatible at  whenfor every tangent parameter . This is the coordinate-free form of\nEvans's compatibility conditions (29)–(30).\n\nA classical solution automatically satisfies them, which shows that\ncompatibility is a necessary condition rather than an artificial one.\n\nSuppose  is differentiable at ,  is differentiable at\n, the trace identity  holds near , and\nthe classical PDE holds at . With , the chain\nrule and the PDE giveThe converse direction, solving for a\ncompatible gradient field from boundary data, uses the implicit function\ntheorem in .\n\nEvaluate the PDE at  to obtain the residual equation. Then\ndifferentiate the trace identity  at ; the chain rule\nidentifies its derivative with\n, which is the tangential compatibility\ncondition.\n\nCompatibility fixes the initial phase state, but for the characteristics\nleaving the surface to fill a neighborhood, the characteristic direction must\nbe transversal to it. That transversality is the noncharacteristic condition,\nformulated as invertibility of the linear map that later turns out to be the\ninitial Jacobian of the spatial characteristic map.\n\nAt a parametrized initial surface, the boundary-tangent directions together\nwith the characteristic direction  define an invertible linear map\nonto the ambient space. In coordinates this is Evans's determinant\ncondition (36).\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Hyperbolic Equations\u0009Nonlinear First-Order Equations\u0009Characteristics and Local Existence","header":"7.4.1.1. Characteristic Equations and Compatible Data","id":"/Hyperbolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Hyperbolic-Equations--Nonlinear-First-Order-Equations--Characteristics-and-Local-Existence--Characteristic-Equations-and-Compatible-Data"},"/Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics/#PDEAtlas--Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics--Euler___-Symmetry___-and-Boussinesq-Flow--Incompressible-Euler-Flow--Exact-solid-rotation":{"contents":"Consider the planar fields\n\n\n\nThe velocity is divergence-free. Its convective acceleration is\n, while , so pressure supplies the\ncentripetal force. This flow is rotational rather than irrotational:\n and its Bernoulli function is .\nIt is constant along the circular streamlines, but not throughout the plane.\n\nWe reuse the polynomial differentiation from Foundations to check a vector\nequation and its pressure constraint. The pressure itself is not harmonic;\nits Laplacian balances the divergence of the convective acceleration. The\nsame velocity has zero strain, which connects this flow to the\nsymmetric-gradient energy example.\n\nThe verified CAS provesThe symbolic calculation produces the differentiated residual, while the\ngenerated theorem proves that the momentum and pressure residuals vanish on\nall of .\n\nThe divergence is . Direct differentiation\ngiveswhich cancels . The checked proof reflects these polynomial\nexpressions, differentiates them symbolically, and closes the normalized\nidentities with cas. It also verifies  and\n.\n\nThe exact momentum proof is a one-line tactic call:\n\n-- Prove the exact vector momentum residual is zero.\nopen CAS.Certificate\nopen NavierStokes.Symbolic\n\nexample :\n    ResidualBound\n      solidRotationEulerMomentumResidual2.vectorFn\n      Set.univ 0 := by\n  cas\n\n\nThe symbolic certificate yields the analytic statementsThese conclusions use the ordinary analytic operators\npartialDeriv, materialDerivative, and spatialGradient.\n\nThe zero-error certificates make each evaluated symbolic residual vanish.\nThe bridge lemmas identify those evaluations with the corresponding analytic\noperators. Since the certified vorticity is the constant , all of its\nspacetime derivatives vanish, giving the transport equation as well.\n\nThe same rigid rotation has zero strain:Thus the velocity gradient is antisymmetric, every strain entry vanishes,\nand the symmetric-gradient energy density is zero.\n\nThe four spatial derivatives are . In each strain component the\noff-diagonal derivatives cancel and the diagonal derivative is zero. The\ncertified energy expression is therefore a sum of zero squares.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Incompressible Fluid Mechanics\u0009Euler, Symmetry, and Boussinesq Flow\u0009Incompressible Euler Flow","header":"8.4.1.7. Exact solid rotation","id":"/Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics/#PDEAtlas--Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics--Euler___-Symmetry___-and-Boussinesq-Flow--Incompressible-Euler-Flow--Exact-solid-rotation"},"/Parabolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations--Bounded-Domains-and-Improved-Regularity--Improved-Time-and-Spatial-Regularity--Regularity-of-Every-Order":{"contents":"The preceding estimates give finite-order regularity. Evans's §7.1.3\ncontinues by induction: if the data are smoother and satisfy more\ncompatibility conditions, every weak solution has more derivatives in both\ntime and space, and with smooth data it is smooth up to the boundary.\n and\n carry this out for the full\noperator\n\n\n\non a bounded open set , , with a\n graph boundary for every . The coefficients , , \nare  up to the boundary for every , the matrix  is\nsymmetric on  with ellipticity constant , and none of\nthem depends on time.\n\nThe compatibility conditions come from the equation itself. If  is\nsmooth, then , and differentiating in time gives the initial\nvalues of all time derivatives in terms of the data alone:\n\n\n\nSince  vanishes on the lateral boundary for all times, so does each\n, and therefore each  must vanish on .\nThis recurrence computes  directly from  and .\n\nFix . There is a constant , depending on , ,\n and the coefficients, with the following property. Letwhere each derivative is the weak time derivative of the previous one, let\n, and suppose that  all lie in\n. Then every weak solution  of  in\n with  on the lateral boundary and \nsatisfiesagain with each derivative the weak time derivative of the previous one, and\n\nThe proof is by induction on . For  it is the  theorem for\nthe full operator:  and .For the step from  to , let  be the weak solution with forcing\n and initial value . The data of  satisfy the\nhypotheses at order , with the compatibility conditions shifted by one,\nso the induction hypothesis gives  its derivatives. The primitive\n solves the original problem, so uniqueness of weak\nsolutions identifies it with , and . At almost every time the\nequation then reads , whose right side lies in\n, and the elliptic estimate of every order lifts  to\n. The norms combine into the stated bound, with the trace constant\nfor  and the operator norm of  entering .The induction works with weak solutions only. After the base case no\nGalerkin approximation is differentiated: the order- theorem is applied\nto the data  directly, and the identification  comes\nfrom uniqueness.\n\nThe forcing derivatives  and the corresponding solution\nderivatives are linked by weak time derivatives.\n\nSmooth data give a smooth solution. The derivative jets of every order\nplace each  in  for every , and the\nSobolev embedding in space and time gives a single function that is smooth\nup to the boundary of the cylinder.\n\nUnder the standing hypotheses on  and , there are constants\n, , chosen before the data, with the following property.\nLet  be  up to the boundary of  for every ,\nlet  be  up to the boundary of  for every  and\ncontinuous on , and suppose that every  vanishes on\n. Then there is a function  with these properties.1.  is continuous on  and  up to the\n   boundary of  for every . It satisfies\n    in ,  on ,\n   and  on .2. For every , the Sobolev norms of its time derivatives obey the bound of\n    with constant .3.  on  for every .4. If  is continuous on the closed cylinder, the equation holds on\n   , end times included.5. Every other function with the properties in item 1 equals  on\n   .6. Every weak solution with data  agrees with  almost everywhere.\n\nThe Galerkin theorem supplies a weak solution , and smooth data satisfy\nthe hypotheses of  at every order.\nFor fixed orders  and , the jets place  in one\ncommon space  with weak time derivatives between\nconsecutive entries. A function whose time derivatives up to order  lie in\n with  large has a representative that is  in space and\ntime up to the boundary. The representatives for different choices agree,\nwhich gives a single . It satisfies the equation, the initial condition\nand the lateral condition pointwise, because it agrees with the weak solution\nalmost everywhere and all three sides are continuous.For uniqueness, a classical solution with data  is also a weak\nsolution with these data. Weak solutions are unique by the energy estimate\nwith the Gårding shift, so every classical solution agrees with  almost\neverywhere, which gives item 6, and by continuity two classical solutions\nagree on the closed cylinder, end times included.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Parabolic Equations\u0009Bounded Domains and Improved Regularity\u0009Improved Time and Spatial Regularity","header":"6.4.3.2. Regularity of Every Order","id":"/Parabolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations--Bounded-Domains-and-Improved-Regularity--Improved-Time-and-Spatial-Regularity--Regularity-of-Every-Order"},"/Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points/#PDEAtlas--Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points--Representing-Solutions--Explicit-Representations-and-Transform-Methods--Separation-of-Variables-and-Spectral-Expansions--Separation-of-variables--Abstract-spectral-expansions":{"contents":"A single separated mode solves the equation but matches only its own initial\ndatum. Representing general data requires expanding in the whole family\n. Here that family is an arbitrary Hilbert basis of an inner\nproduct space  over  or .\n\nFor any Hilbert basis  and any vector , the Fourier coefficients\n reconstruct u: the net of finite sums\n converges to u in the Hilbert norm. This\nis Mathlib's basis theorem behind Evans's eigenfunction-series method.\n\nMathlib defines a Hilbert basis by its coefficient map, a linear isometric\nequivalence onto the square-summable sequences; it sends  to the sequence\nof its coefficients. Such a sequence is the sum, in the sequence norm, of its\nsingle-entry pieces. The inverse isometry is continuous and maps each piece to\nthe coefficient times the basis vector in , so the expansion converges to .\n\nThe expansion converges as a net indexed by finite subsets of the index set.\nSpectral methods truncate an -indexed basis to the initial\nsegments , and this unconditional convergence covers them in\nparticular.\n\nFor a basis indexed by the natural numbers, the conventional first-N\npartial sums converge in norm:\n\nThe basis expansion gives convergence of the net over all finite index sets.\nThe initial segments  eventually contain every fixed finite\nset. Restricting the convergent net to these initial segments gives\n in norm.\n\nNorm convergence of the expansion is the qualitative statement; Parseval's\nidentity is its quantitative counterpart, obtained by taking the squared norm\non both sides and using orthonormality to remove the cross terms. The\ncoefficients are written  with the basis vector in the\nfirst slot, following Mathlib's convention for which argument is\nconjugate-linear.\n\nThe squared Hilbert norm is exactly the sum of the squared norms of the\nHilbert-basis coefficients:\n\nMathlib's coefficient map is a linear isometry onto the square-summable\nsequences, and the coefficient of each index is the inner product with that\nbasis vector. The squared norm of a square-summable sequence is the sum of the\nsquared norms of its entries. Applying this to the coefficient sequence and\nusing that the isometry preserves norms yields Parseval's identity.\n\nSubtracting the first  terms from  leaves a vector whose nonzero\ncoefficients are exactly the omitted ones, so Parseval applied to that\ndifference converts the truncation error into a coefficient sum.\n\nThe squared error of the first-N approximation is exactly the sum of the\nsquared omitted coefficients:This is the quantitative identity behind\nspectral truncation and Galerkin approximation estimates.\n\nSplit the series of squared coefficient norms after its first  terms; the\ntail is indexed by . Orthonormality makes the squared truncation error\nequal to the squared norm minus the retained squared coefficients, and Parseval\nidentifies the squared norm with the full series. Hence, with ,\nthe error equals the tail, which is the displayed formula.\n\nThe tail identity by itself gives no rate, since it only rearranges the\ncoefficients. A rate appears once the coefficients are known to decay: if\n is finite, then every\nomitted index satisfies , so the monotone weight  can be\npulled out of the tail and compared termwise with the weighted energy. For\neigenfunction expansions such weighted summability is a regularity statement\nabout , expressed spectrally.\n\nFor , if the coefficient energy weighted by  is summable, then the\nfirst-N squared truncation error is bounded by that weighted energy divided\nby .\n\nMultiply the exact omitted tail by the monotone weight at index N, compare\nit termwise with the weighted tail, and divide by the strictly positive\nquantity .\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Representation Formulas, Variational Principles, and Fixed Points\u0009Representing Solutions\u0009Explicit Representations and Transform Methods\u0009Separation of Variables and Spectral Expansions\u0009Separation of variables","header":"9.2.1.3.1.1. Abstract spectral expansions","id":"/Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points/#PDEAtlas--Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points--Representing-Solutions--Explicit-Representations-and-Transform-Methods--Separation-of-Variables-and-Spectral-Expansions--Separation-of-variables--Abstract-spectral-expansions"}});